<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/nutrition-for-performance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Sports nutrition is the nutrition lesson's physiology, <strong>aimed at performance
    instead of health alone</strong>: fuel the session, repair after, hydrate through —
    with the evidence tables the supplement aisle wishes it had. The engine-room
    lessons established what the body burns (glycogen for intensity, fat for the
    long haul, ATP as the currency); nutrition is the procurement department,
    and its discipline is arithmetic plus honesty: how much, of what, when — and
    which of the thousands of products on the shelf have any evidence behind
    them. This page covers energy balance as athletes actually experience it,
    the macronutrient prescriptions with real numbers, carbohydrate
    periodisation and race fuelling, protein and the recovery question, hydration
    done honestly (including the error that killed marathon runners), the
    supplement evidence tier list, and RED-S — the modern warning about
    underfuelling.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="balance, protein, carbohydrate, hydration" widget="equationStep" config={nutriCfg} />

  <h2>Diet and energy sources: the macronutrient arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Energy balance still governs body mass — calories in versus out is
    thermodynamics — but an athlete's "out" is bigger and more variable: a 70 kg
    runner training 90 minutes daily burns perhaps 3,000–3,500 kcal against a
    sedentary 2,000–2,400, and the metabolism partly adapts (reducing
    non-exercise movement under chronic deficit, one reason "eat less, move
    more" underperforms in practice). The three macronutrients then divide the
    budget by function. <strong>Carbohydrate</strong> (4 kcal/g) is the high-intensity
    fuel: it stocks muscle and liver glycogen — the tank the energy-systems
    lesson sized at roughly 1,600–2,000 kcal. <strong>Fat</strong> (9 kcal/g) is the
    abundant low-intensity fuel, essential for hormone production, and
    effectively unlimited in storage even in lean athletes.
    <strong>Protein</strong> (4 kcal/g) is structural — amino acids for repair and
    growth — and a poor fuel, used appreciably only when carbohydrate runs
    short. The professional prescriptions are per kilogram, not percentages:
    carbohydrate 3–5 g/kg/day on easy days rising to 5–10 g/kg/day for heavy
    endurance training; protein 1.2–1.6 g/kg/day for endurance athletes and
    <strong>1.6–2.2 g/kg/day</strong> for those training for strength and hypertrophy
    (the meta-analytic consensus; more than that adds little); fat filling the
    remainder, rarely below about 1 g/kg/day since chronic low fat intake
    degrades hormones. <strong>A worked day:</strong> our 70 kg runner in a heavy
    block targets 5–7 g/kg carbohydrate (350–490 g), 1.4 g/kg protein (~100 g)
    and the balance as fat — roughly 3,200 kcal, which is why "small healthy
    meals" fail athletes: the volume has to exist somewhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Carbohydrate periodisation and race fuelling</h2>
  <p>
    Because glycogen is the limiting tank for anything intense or longer than
    about 90 minutes, carbohydrate strategy is periodised like training itself.
    <strong>Daily fuelling</strong> matches the load (the arithmetic above);
    <strong>pre-event</strong>, a carbohydrate-focused meal 2–4 hours before tops the
    tank, and the classic <strong>carbo-loading</strong> for events beyond 90 minutes
    (2–3 days at 8–12 g/kg/day while tapering) can raise glycogen stores
    20–40% — the original endurance tactic, still valid. <strong>Intra-event</strong>,
    the rules are duration-driven: for sessions under about 60–75 minutes,
    water suffices; beyond 90 minutes, exogenous carbohydrate at roughly
    <strong>60–90 g/h</strong> — using mixed glucose and fructose sources, because
    the two sugars use different intestinal transporters (glucose via SGLT1,
    fructose via GLUT5), so the mixture is absorbed faster than either alone
    and spares liver glycogen. The gut itself is trainable: athletes
    practising high in-race carbohydrate rates adapt to absorb more and
    suffer less distress, which is why "I can't stomach gels" is a training
    problem, not a permanent verdict. <strong>Recovery</strong> softened from folklore
    to evidence: the urgent "30-minute anabolic window" dissolved once
    studies showed total daily intake dominating — but the 24-hour
    replenishment clock is real, so same-day carbohydrate (about 1–1.2 g/kg/h
    in the hours after exhaustive work, when next-day training matters)
    remains the practical rule.
  </p>

  <h2>Protein: the repair budget and its timing</h2>
  <p>
    Protein's job in sports nutrition is funding muscle-protein synthesis —
    the repair-and-rebuild cycle that training switches on. The research
    picture, refined across two decades: <strong>total daily intake</strong> matters
    most (1.6–2.2 g/kg/day for strength goals); <strong>distribution</strong> matters
    modestly — dividing intake across 3–4 meals of roughly 0.3–0.4 g/kg each
    keeps each meal above the "leucine threshold" (about 2–3 g of leucine,
    the trigger amino acid) that maximally stimulates synthesis per meal;
    and <strong>timing</strong> around training is a fine-tuning, not a cliff edge —
    the classic post-workout window extends over hours, so a protein meal
    within a couple of sessions' distance of training is sufficient. Whole
    food proteins (dairy, eggs, meat, legumes) outperform isolated
    supplements on satiety and micronutrients, though whey remains a
    convenient, fast-absorbing option. One correction the evidence made:
    <strong>branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) supplements are redundant</strong> for
    anyone eating adequate protein — the complete protein already contains
    them, in better company.
  </p>

  <h2>Hydration and sodium, honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Fluid balance is a performance variable with a symmetric failure mode.
    <strong>Dehydration</strong> of about <strong>2% of body mass</strong> measurably degrades
    endurance performance (more in heat), so the practical protocols:
    train mostly <strong>drinking to thirst</strong>; in hot or long sessions,
    weigh before and after — each kilogram lost is roughly a litre of
    sweat to replace; and pre-hydrate before known hot competition.
    <strong>Sodium</strong> matters beyond about two hours of heavy sweating
    (sweat is salty; water alone eventually dilutes the blood), which is
    why long-event fuel includes electrolytes. But the field's hardest
    lesson was that <strong>overhydration is the rarer and worse error</strong>:
    exercise-associated <strong>hyponatraemia</strong> — blood sodium diluted to
    dangerous levels by drinking large volumes of plain water during long,
    slow endurance events — hospitalises dozens of marathon runners a
    year and has killed them. The old advice ("drink before you're
    thirsty", aiming to match or exceed sweat loss) produced the
    epidemic; modern guidance inverts it — drink to thirst, replace
    sodium in long events, and accept modest dehydration as far less
    dangerous than its overcorrection.
  </p>

  <h2>The supplement tier list</h2>
  <p>
    The supplement market is unregulated enough that marketing outruns
    evidence almost everywhere — which is why evidence-graded frameworks
    like the <strong>Australian Institute of Sport's ABCD system</strong> matter:
    Group A holds supplements with strong proof, and it is short.
    <strong>Creatine monohydrate</strong> — the most-studied supplement in sports
    science — improves repeated high-intensity performance (~3–5 g/day,
    no loading required, no credible harms at standard doses).
    <strong>Caffeine</strong> at 3–6 mg/kg (210–420 mg for a 70 kg athlete — a
    strong coffee is roughly 100 mg), taken about 60 minutes before,
    reliably improves endurance performance and lowers perceived effort.
    <strong>Nitrate</strong> (beetroot juice) shows moderate benefits for
    sub-elite endurance performance; <strong>beta-alanine</strong> (buffering,
    3–6 g/day for weeks) helps events of roughly 1–4 minutes;
    <strong>sodium bicarbonate</strong> works but punishes the gut. Everything
    else descends through "emerging" and "peripheral" to Group D
    ("banned or at high risk of contamination"). Two cautions the
    professionals add: supplements are a <em>topping</em> — worth a percent
    or two only on top of fuelling, training and sleep done right; and
    contamination is a documented doping-positive route, so tested
    athletes use third-party certified products (Informed Sport,
    NSF Certified for Sport) — under anti-doping's strict liability,
    what is in the tub is the athlete's responsibility regardless of
    the label.
  </p>

  <h2>RED-S: when the fuelling fails</h2>
  <p>
    The modern warning label of sports nutrition is <strong>RED-S</strong> —
    Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport: the cascade of harm from chronic
    energy intake that falls short of training expenditure. Its precursor,
    the "female athlete triad" (disordered eating, menstrual dysfunction,
    low bone density), generalised once male athletes proved equally
    susceptible. The mechanism is energy availability — dietary energy
    minus exercise energy, per kg of fat-free mass; below roughly
    30 kcal/kg fat-free mass per day (the healthy floor is around 45),
    the body budgets like a famine: reproductive hormones shut down
    (lost periods in women, low testosterone in men), bone formation
    stalls (stress fractures — the classic presentation in distance
    runners and gymnasts), immunity and recovery degrade. The culture
    that causes it is the "lighter is faster" myth: in reality,
    performance declines below the energy floor even as body mass
    falls — the runner gets slower <em>and</em> sicker. The cure is
    fuelling up and, where needed, psychological support for the
    disordered-eating component; the prevention is education, which
    is one reason this lesson exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Fuelling by event: three worked days</h2>
  <p>
    Prescriptions become real when they are priced onto an athlete. <strong>The
    marathoner (70 kg, heavy block):</strong> carbohydrate 6–8 g/kg = 420–560 g
    across the day (oats and banana pre-run, rice-based meals, simple carbs
    in the session); protein 1.4 g/kg ≈ 100 g (spread over four meals of
    25 g); fluid driven by the weighing-in protocol (each hot 90-minute
    session costing 1.5 kg = 1.5 L to replace, with sodium in anything
    beyond 90 minutes). <strong>The strength athlete (80 kg):</strong> protein
    first — 2.0 g/kg = 160 g, four feedings of 40 g; carbohydrate around
    sessions (4–6 g/kg = 320–480 g on training days, less on rest days);
    total energy above maintenance while gaining, because a surplus is
    the growth lever the protein merely equips. <strong>The footballer
    (75 kg, match day plus travel):</strong> the match-day script — high
    carbohydrate the previous day (7–8 g/kg), pre-match meal 3 hours
    out, in-match carbohydrate at ~30–60 g/h for the second half,
    recovery carbs plus 25 g protein within a couple of hours — and
    the travel problem: catering control away from home is where
    periodised fuelling usually dies. Three days, one template:
    carbohydrate scaled to the day's engine work, protein scaled to
    repair, fluid scaled to sweat — everything else is garnish.
  </p>

  <h2>The quiet levers: sleep, alcohol and travel</h2>
  <p>
    Three factors sit outside the macro tables and inside every result.
    <strong>Sleep</strong> is the recovery input everything else assumes: restriction
    (under about 7 hours) measurably reduces glycogen resynthesis,
    impairs sprint accuracy and raises injury rates in team-sport
    studies — so sleep hygiene (regular times, dark cool rooms,
    caffeine cut-off eight hours before) is nutrition policy by
    other means. <strong>Alcohol</strong> earns its caution empirically:
    after exercise it impairs muscle-protein synthesis and
    rehydration (it is a diuretic, and it blunts the drive to
    refuel), so "the post-match pint" competes directly with
    the recovery window the same evening needs.
    <strong>Travel</strong> adds jet lag — circadian disruption that
    degrades performance for days (the rule of thumb: about one
    day per time zone to adapt, helped by light exposure timing)
    — and appetite drift: long-haul athletes under-eat precisely
    when energy needs are high, which is where RED-S risk
    quietly accumulates. None of these appear on a plate,
    and all three decide whether the plate's arithmetic
    reaches the muscle.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Train-low, compete-high.</strong> Deliberately training with low
    glycogen (fasted sessions, "sleep low" protocols) amplifies
    fat-oxidation signalling — and the performance evidence remains
    mixed, with most controlled trials finding no endurance benefit
    over well-fuelled training. Its niche use is as a periodic
    stressor for metabolic flexibility, not a lifestyle; competing
    low is simply slower.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The gut as a trainable organ.</strong> Systematic "gut training"
    (progressively raising in-session carbohydrate to 90+ g/h)
    measurably increases absorption capacity and reduces distress —
    one of the field's genuinely new findings of the last decade.
    Alongside it: individual sweat-sodium testing (sweat sodium
    concentration varies several-fold between people) is making
    fluid plans personalised rather than formulaic. The open
    question is the low-energy-availability threshold — where
    exactly health risk begins for each athlete, and how early
    bone loss reverses.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AIS Sports Supplement Framework; ACSM/AND/DC position stands on nutrition and athletic performance; IOC consensus on RED-S; the training and thermodynamics lessons.</p>

